    outlook: "The outlook for the remainder of 2026 and into 2027 remains highly contingent on geopolitical developments in the Strait of Hormuz. Prediction markets suggest a less than 50% chance of normalization by mid-2027, implying a prolonged risk premium in oil markets. While central banks are likely to maintain a cautious stance, any sustained oil price spike could force a more hawkish tilt, especially in the U.S. Equities are expected to remain volatile, with energy-heavy indices facing the most immediate pressure. Defensive positioning, selective hedging, and monitoring of geopolitical headlines will be critical for investors navigating this uncertain landscape. The potential for a diplomatic breakthrough remains a wildcard, but the structural risk of prolonged disruption has increased significantly.",
    conclusion: "The Strait of Hormuz is not just a regional chokepoint—it is a global risk multiplier. With prediction markets assigning low odds to a swift resolution, the geopolitical risk premium has become a permanent fixture in energy markets and investor psychology. While oil prices may ebb and flow, the underlying vulnerability of 20% of global oil supply remains. For investors, the message is clear: adapt to volatility, diversify defensively, and prepare for a market environment where oil is not just a commodity, but a geopolitical weapon. The next 12 months will define how global markets price risk, allocate capital, and navigate a world where energy security is no longer a given.",
